---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BrandLogos from '../../components/BrandLogos.astro';
import FlipWords from '../../components/FlipWords.astro';
import CategoryBlogFeed from '../../components/CategoryBlogFeed.astro';

const title = 'Bomb Blast Window Film Philadelphia | Blast Mitigation';
const description = 'Bomb blast window film in Philadelphia. GSA and ASTM-rated blast mitigation film for commercial, government, and high-occupancy buildings. Free assessment.';
const heroImage = '/images/bomb-blast-window-film-philadelphia-hero.jpg';

const faqItems = [
  { question: 'What is blast mitigation window film and how does it work?', answer: 'Blast mitigation window film is a heavy-duty security film applied to glass that holds shattered glass fragments in place after a blast event. In an explosion, the primary cause of injury from glass is not the blast wave itself but the glass fragments propelled outward at high velocity. Blast mitigation film bonds to the glass and retains fragments in the film matrix when the glass breaks — dramatically reducing the projectile fragment hazard to occupants inside the building.' },
  { question: 'What is the GSA TS01-2003 standard?', answer: 'GSA TS01-2003 is the U.S. General Services Administration\'s standard test method for measuring the resistance of window systems to blast loading. The standard defines test conditions, measurement criteria, and performance classifications (No Break, No Hazard, Minimal Hazard, Very Low Hazard) for glazing systems subjected to blast pressure. Films tested to GSA TS01-2003 have demonstrated documented blast fragment retention performance under standardized blast loading conditions.' },
  { question: 'Does blast film work on existing glass?', answer: 'Yes. Blast mitigation film is a retrofit solution applied to existing glass without glass replacement. This makes it significantly more cost-effective than replacing standard glass with laminated safety glass or blast-resistant glazing systems. The film is applied to the interior glass surface and adheres with pressure-sensitive adhesive. An edge attachment system bonds the film at the perimeter of the frame — critical for maximizing blast resistance performance.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between blast film and standard security film?', answer: 'Standard security or safety film (2–4 mil) is designed for fragment retention after physical impact — forced entry, vandalism, accidents. Blast mitigation film (8–14 mil) is engineered for the specific load profile of a blast event — a high-pressure wave followed by a negative pressure phase that pulls glass outward. The heavier mil weight and pressure-sensitive adhesive system of blast film are calibrated for this dynamic loading pattern, not just static or impact resistance.' },
  { question: 'Is blast mitigation film required in Philadelphia?', answer: 'Blast mitigation film requirements for Philadelphia buildings depend on the building type, occupancy, tenant profile, and applicable federal guidelines. Buildings housing federal tenants, high-value financial institutions, embassies or consulates, and certain government facilities may have explicit blast protection requirements under GSA or Department of Defense standards. Insurance carriers for high-occupancy buildings increasingly recommend blast mitigation as part of comprehensive security hardening. Our team can advise on requirements applicable to your specific building and tenant profile.' },
  { question: 'What buildings in Philadelphia most need bomb blast window film?', answer: 'The highest-priority Philadelphia applications for blast mitigation include federal and government buildings, financial institutions and Class A office towers with high-profile tenants, hotels and convention centers with large public lobbies, hospitals and medical centers with emergency department exposure, transit hubs and transportation infrastructure, and high-density retail and hospitality venues in high-foot-traffic corridors.' },
  { question: 'Does blast mitigation film also provide security against forced entry?', answer: 'Yes. At 8–14 mil thickness, blast mitigation films provide significant forced entry resistance in addition to blast fragment retention. The same properties that hold glass together under blast loading — film weight, adhesive bond strength, and edge attachment — also dramatically slow forced entry attempts through treated glass. For facilities with both blast and forced entry concerns, blast mitigation film addresses both with a single installation.' },
  { question: 'What is the attachment system and why does it matter?', answer: 'The attachment system bonds the film to the window frame at the glass perimeter, preventing the glass-film unit from pulling away from the frame under blast negative pressure. Without an attachment system, the film may hold the glass fragments together but the entire glass unit can be expelled from the frame. With the attachment system properly specified and installed, the glass-film unit remains in the frame opening — maintaining the building envelope integrity after a blast event.' },
  { question: 'How long does blast mitigation film installation take?', answer: 'We do not provide installation time estimates as they depend on the number and size of glass panels, access requirements, building height, whether an attachment system is required, and the film specification. We provide a realistic project timeline in your written proposal after the site assessment. Most commercial installations are completed with minimal disruption to building operations.' },
  { question: 'How do I get blast mitigation window film installed in Philadelphia?', answer: 'Contact us for a free site assessment. We evaluate your building\'s glass exposure, identify priority glazing zones (lobby, ground floor, blast-facing facades), and recommend the appropriate film specification and attachment system. We provide GSA TS01-2003 and ASTM F1642 compliance documentation for permit and insurance purposes. Our team has experience with government facility requirements and can support your approval process.' }
];

const pageSchemas = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://windowfilmphiladelphia.net/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Benefits", "item": "https://windowfilmphiladelphia.net/benefits/" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Blast Mitigation Window Film", "item": "https://windowfilmphiladelphia.net/benefits/blast-mitigation/" }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "WebPage",
    "@id": "https://windowfilmphiladelphia.net/benefits/blast-mitigation/",
    "name": title,
    "description": description,
    "dateModified": new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10),
    "speakable": { "@type": "SpeakableSpecification", "cssSelector": [".bm-hero__title", ".bm-why__headline", ".swt-faq__a"] }
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "ItemList",
    "name": "Blast Mitigation Window Film Types — Philadelphia",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "3M Scotchshield Ultra S800", "description": "8 mil security film tested to GSA TS01-2003 blast standard. Holds glass fragments after detonation." },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Solar Gard Armorcoat with Attachment", "description": "Heavy-duty blast mitigation film with edge attachment system. Tested to ASTM F1642 glazing standard." },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Madico Clearshield Safety", "description": "Multi-layer blast mitigation film for government and high-security facilities. DOD and GSA compliant applications." }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Product",
    "name": "Blast Mitigation Window Film Philadelphia",
    "description": "GSA and ASTM-rated bomb blast window film for Philadelphia commercial, government, hotel, and high-occupancy buildings. Holds glass in place after blast events — reducing fragment injuries.",
    "brand": { "@type": "Brand", "name": "Window Film Philadelphia" },
    "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "availability": "https://schema.org/InStock", "areaServed": "Philadelphia, PA" },
  }
];

const checkIcon = '<svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M8 12l3 3 5-5" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogImage={heroImage}
  serviceName="Blast Mitigation Window Film"
  serviceDesc="GSA and ASTM-rated blast mitigation film for Philadelphia commercial, government, hotel, hospital, and high-occupancy buildings."
  serviceUrl="https://windowfilmphiladelphia.net/benefits/blast-mitigation/"
  faqItems={faqItems}
  schemas={pageSchemas}
>
  <section class="bm-hero">
    <div class="bm-hero__bg" style={`background-image: url('${heroImage}')`} role="img" aria-label="Bomb blast window film in Philadelphia installed on a commercial office tower"></div>
    <div class="bm-hero__overlay"></div>
    <div class="bm-hero__content">
      <div class="bm-hero__eyebrow">
        <span>Philadelphia Blast Protection</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="bm-hero__title">Bomb Blast Window Film in Philadelphia</h1>
      <p class="bm-hero__animated-line">
        <FlipWords
          words={["for Office Towers", "for Government Facilities", "for Hotels and Convention Centers", "for Hospitals and Medical Centers"]}
          staticText=""
          class="bm-hero__flip"
        />
      </p>
      <p class="bm-hero__subtitle">GSA and ASTM-rated blast mitigation film that holds glass in place after a blast event — protecting Philadelphia building occupants from fragment injury without replacing your glass.</p>
      <div class="bm-hero__cta">
        <a href="/contact/" class="bm-hero__btn-primary">Get a Free Assessment</a>
        <a href="tel:2673947980" class="bm-hero__btn-secondary">Call (267) 394-7980</a>
      </div>
      <div class="bm-hero__stats-panel" aria-label="Blast mitigation film statistics">
        <div class="bm-hero__stats-inner">
          <div class="bm-hero__stat"><span class="bm-hero__stat-number">8–14 mil</span><span class="bm-hero__stat-label">Blast Film Thickness</span></div>
          <div class="bm-hero__stat-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="bm-hero__stat"><span class="bm-hero__stat-number">GSA TS01-2003</span><span class="bm-hero__stat-label">Blast Standard</span></div>
          <div class="bm-hero__stat-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="bm-hero__stat"><span class="bm-hero__stat-number">ASTM F1642</span><span class="bm-hero__stat-label">Glazing Standard</span></div>
          <div class="bm-hero__stat-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>
          <div class="bm-hero__stat"><span class="bm-hero__stat-number">No Glass</span><span class="bm-hero__stat-label">Replacement Required</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="swt-body">
    <section class="swt-trust">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="swt-trust__label">Trusted by Philadelphia office towers, government facilities, hotels, hospitals, and high-occupancy commercial buildings.</p>
        <BrandLogos />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bm-why" id="problem">
      <div class="bm-why__container">
        <div class="bm-why__image-wrap">
          <img src="/images/bomb-blast-window-film-philadelphia-threat.jpg" alt="Center City commercial tower protected with bomb blast window film in Philadelphia" title="Bomb blast window film in Philadelphia for Center City commercial glass" class="bm-why__image" loading="lazy" />
          <div class="bm-why__stat-card">
            <p>Glass fragment injuries account for up to 90% of casualties from blast events in buildings — Whole Building Design Guide</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bm-why__content">
          <div class="bm-why__eyebrow">The Risk</div>
          <h2 class="bm-why__headline">Unprotected Glass Is the Primary Blast Hazard in Any Building</h2>
          <ul class="bm-why__bullets">
            <li set:html={`${checkIcon}<span>In a blast event, standard glass shatters and propels fragments at high velocity — the primary cause of occupant injury is not the blast wave but the glass.</span>`}></li>
            <li set:html={`${checkIcon}<span>Blast mitigation film in Philadelphia is applied to existing glass and holds shattered fragments in the film matrix — preventing the glass from becoming a projectile hazard.</span>`}></li>
            <li set:html={`${checkIcon}<span>Philadelphia's dense urban environment, federal building presence, high-profile hospitality, and international business profile create meaningful blast risk across the Class A office and government facility portfolio.</span>`}></li>
            <li set:html={`${checkIcon}<span>GSA and Department of Defense guidelines increasingly require blast mitigation for any building housing federal tenants or high-value occupancies.</span>`}></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="bm-why__closing">"Laminated glass and window film are among the most cost-effective methods to reduce glass-fragment hazards from explosions in existing buildings," according to the <a href="https://www.wbdg.org/resources/windowsecurity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Whole Building Design Guide — Window Security</a>. GSA TS01-2003 classifies blast glazing performance on a scale from "No Hazard" to "High Hazard," and Window Film Philadelphia specifies 8–14 mil film with perimeter attachment to achieve a "Minimal Hazard" or "Low Hazard" rating on existing commercial glass. For broader forced-entry concerns, see our <a href="/benefits/safety-and-security/">commercial safety and security window film</a> page. Buildings with high solar load may also benefit from reviewing our <a href="/products/3m-window-film/">3M window film products</a>, some of which combine solar control with security performance.</p>
          <a href="/contact/" class="bm-why__cta">Schedule a blast risk assessment</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bm-slider" id="bmSlider" aria-label="Blast mitigation film products">
      <div class="bm-slider__track" id="bmTrack">
        <div class="bm-slider__slide" data-slide="0">
          <img src="/images/blast-mitigation-film-philadelphia-scotchshield.jpg" alt="3M Scotchshield Ultra S800 blast mitigation film on Philadelphia office glass" title="Scotchshield Ultra S800 blast mitigation film Philadelphia" class="bm-slider__bg" />
          <div class="bm-slider__overlay"></div>
          <div class="bm-slider__content">
            <div class="bm-slider__step">01</div>
            <h2 class="bm-slider__title">Scotchshield Ultra S800. GSA TS01-2003 Rated.</h2>
            <p class="bm-slider__desc">3M Scotchshield Ultra S800 is an 8 mil optically clear blast mitigation film tested to GSA TS01-2003 — the U.S. General Services Administration's standard for blast-resistant window systems. It achieves a "Minimal Hazard" rating under GSA TS01-2003 when properly installed with an attachment system. The film holds glass fragments in place after a blast event, preventing them from becoming projectile hazards for occupants inside the building. 3M Scotchshield Ultra S800 — 8 mil, tested GSA TS01-2003 Minimal Hazard rating with proper attachment system installation.</p>
            <div class="bm-slider__faq">
              <div class="bm-slider__faq-item"><button class="bm-slider__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What does "Minimal Hazard" mean in GSA testing?<span class="bm-slider__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="bm-slider__faq-a" hidden>Minimal Hazard is a GSA TS01-2003 performance classification showing that fragments are retained near the window opening rather than projected deep into the occupied area.</div></div>
              <div class="bm-slider__faq-item"><button class="bm-slider__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does S800 require an attachment system?<span class="bm-slider__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="bm-slider__faq-a" hidden>For documented blast performance, yes. The attachment system bonds the film-glass unit to the frame so the system can resist both overpressure and the negative pressure phase.</div></div>
              <div class="bm-slider__faq-item"><button class="bm-slider__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Can S800 be applied to retrofit existing glass?<span class="bm-slider__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="bm-slider__faq-a" hidden>Yes. S800 is designed for retrofit installation on existing commercial glazing — no glass replacement required. It is the most widely specified blast film for Philadelphia commercial projects.</div></div>
            </div>
            <a href="/contact/" class="bm-slider__cta">Get a Free Assessment</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bm-slider__slide" data-slide="1">
          <img src="/images/bomb-blast-film-philadelphia-armorcoat.jpg" alt="Solar Gard Armorcoat blast film with attachment system on Philadelphia commercial glass" title="Solar Gard Armorcoat ASTM F1642 bomb blast film Philadelphia" class="bm-slider__bg" />
          <div class="bm-slider__overlay"></div>
          <div class="bm-slider__content">
            <div class="bm-slider__step">02</div>
            <h2 class="bm-slider__title">Armorcoat. Heavy Mil. ASTM F1642 Tested.</h2>
            <p class="bm-slider__desc">Solar Gard Armorcoat blast mitigation film is available in 8 and 14 mil weights, tested to ASTM F1642, the standard specification for glazing and glazing systems subjected to airblast loadings. The Armorcoat installation includes a perimeter attachment system that bonds the film-glass unit to the window frame — critical for preventing glass expulsion from the frame opening during the negative pressure phase of a blast event. For Philadelphia buildings where maximum fragment retention and frame integrity are required, Armorcoat with attachment is the specification of choice.</p>
            <div class="bm-slider__faq">
              <div class="bm-slider__faq-item"><button class="bm-slider__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What is ASTM F1642?<span class="bm-slider__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="bm-slider__faq-a" hidden>ASTM F1642 is a glazing performance standard for systems subjected to airblast loadings. It is used to evaluate how glass, film, and attachment systems retain fragments under blast pressure.</div></div>
              <div class="bm-slider__faq-item"><button class="bm-slider__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What does the attachment system do?<span class="bm-slider__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="bm-slider__faq-a" hidden>The attachment system secures the treated glass to the frame so the glass-film unit is less likely to pull out of the opening when the blast wave reverses.</div></div>
              <div class="bm-slider__faq-item"><button class="bm-slider__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What is the difference between 8 and 14 mil Armorcoat?<span class="bm-slider__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="bm-slider__faq-a" hidden>Both are heavy security films, but the 14 mil specification adds mass and tear resistance for higher-risk exposures. We specify the weight after evaluating glass size, frame type, and risk profile.</div></div>
            </div>
            <a href="/contact/" class="bm-slider__cta">Get a Free Assessment</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bm-slider__slide" data-slide="2">
          <img src="/images/blast-mitigation-window-film-philadelphia-government.jpg" alt="Madico Clearshield blast mitigation film for Philadelphia government building" title="Madico Clearshield government blast mitigation film Philadelphia" class="bm-slider__bg" />
          <div class="bm-slider__overlay"></div>
          <div class="bm-slider__content">
            <div class="bm-slider__step">03</div>
            <h2 class="bm-slider__title">Madico Clearshield. Government Proven.</h2>
            <p class="bm-slider__desc">Madico Clearshield is a multi-layer blast mitigation film with documented installations across DOD and GSA facilities. Its multi-layer construction is designed specifically for the dynamic loading profile of a blast event — the combination of overpressure and negative pressure that standard single-layer films are not optimized for. For Philadelphia government facilities, federal tenant buildings, and high-value commercial properties requiring maximum documented blast performance, Madico Clearshield provides a proven government-grade specification.</p>
            <div class="bm-slider__faq">
              <div class="bm-slider__faq-item"><button class="bm-slider__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What is the difference between single-layer and multi-layer blast film?<span class="bm-slider__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="bm-slider__faq-a" hidden>Multi-layer systems are built to distribute force through several layers of polyester and adhesive, improving fragment retention under dynamic blast loading.</div></div>
              <div class="bm-slider__faq-item"><button class="bm-slider__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">Does Madico comply with GSA requirements?<span class="bm-slider__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="bm-slider__faq-a" hidden>Madico Clearshield has documented government facility use and can be specified with the required performance documentation for GSA-oriented projects.</div></div>
              <div class="bm-slider__faq-item"><button class="bm-slider__faq-q" aria-expanded="false">What documentation is provided?<span class="bm-slider__faq-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="bm-slider__faq-a" hidden>We provide manufacturer specification sheets, installation records, product warranty terms, and compliance documentation applicable to the selected film and attachment system.</div></div>
            </div>
            <a href="/contact/" class="bm-slider__cta">Get a Free Assessment</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <nav class="bm-slider__nav" aria-label="Slide navigation">
        <button class="bm-slider__nav-item bm-slider__nav-item--active" data-bmnav="0"><span class="bm-slider__nav-line"></span><span class="bm-slider__nav-label">S800</span></button>
        <button class="bm-slider__nav-item" data-bmnav="1"><span class="bm-slider__nav-line"></span><span class="bm-slider__nav-label">Armorcoat</span></button>
        <button class="bm-slider__nav-item" data-bmnav="2"><span class="bm-slider__nav-line"></span><span class="bm-slider__nav-label">Clearshield</span></button>
      </nav>
      <button class="bm-slider__arrow bm-slider__arrow--prev" id="bmPrev" aria-label="Previous slide">‹</button>
      <button class="bm-slider__arrow bm-slider__arrow--next" id="bmNext" aria-label="Next slide">›</button>
      <div class="bm-slider__progress"><div class="bm-slider__progress-bar" id="bmProgressBar"></div></div>
    </section>

    <section class="bm-bento" id="protection">
      <div class="bm-bento__container">
        <div class="bm-bento__header">
          <div class="bm-bento__eyebrow">Fragment Retention</div>
          <h2 class="bm-bento__headline">How does blast film change the failure pattern?</h2>
          <p class="bm-bento__sub">Blast mitigation film is selected around the physics of glass failure: fragment velocity, frame pullout, and the negative pressure phase that follows the initial blast wave.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bm-bento__grid">
          <div class="bm-bento-card bm-bento-card--hero">
            <div class="bm-bento-card__hero-icon" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg width="58" height="58" viewBox="0 0 58 58" fill="none"><path d="M29 5l17 7v13c0 12-7 22-17 28-10-6-17-16-17-28V12l17-7z" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M17 31h24M20 21l18 18M38 21L20 39" stroke="#7CB342" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
            </div>
            <h3 class="bm-bento-card__hero-title">The Physics of Blast Hazard — and How Film Stops It</h3>
            <p class="bm-bento-card__hero-text">Glass fragments propelled by blast events travel at velocities exceeding 100 mph — blast mitigation film holds them in the film matrix, preventing occupant injury. The film and attachment system turn uncontrolled fragments into a retained glass-film unit.</p>
            <div class="bm-bento-card__pill">Fragment Retention Technology</div>
          </div>
          <div class="bm-bento-card bm-bento-card--photo">
            <img src="/images/bomb-blast-window-film-philadelphia-commercial.jpg" alt="Commercial high-rise protected with bomb blast window film in Philadelphia" title="Commercial high-rise bomb blast window film in Philadelphia" loading="lazy" class="bm-bento-card__img" />
            <div class="bm-bento-card__photo-overlay"></div>
            <div class="bm-bento-card__photo-content"><h3>Commercial High-Rise Glass</h3><p>Lobby, podium, and ground-floor zones get priority treatment.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="bm-bento-card bm-bento-card--photo">
            <img src="/images/blast-mitigation-film-philadelphia-office-glass.jpg" alt="Office glass treated for blast mitigation in a Philadelphia commercial building" title="Philadelphia office glass blast mitigation film" loading="lazy" class="bm-bento-card__img" />
            <div class="bm-bento-card__photo-overlay"></div>
            <div class="bm-bento-card__photo-content"><h3>Priority Glazing Zones</h3><p>Blast-facing facades, entries, and public-facing glass are evaluated first.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div class="bm-bento-card bm-bento-card--dark-accent">
            <h3>GSA TS01-2003 &amp; ASTM F1642 Rated</h3>
            <p>Specifications are selected from products tested to government and glazing blast standards, with the correct attachment method documented for the building record.</p>
            <span class="bm-bento-card__micro-pill">Compliance Documentation</span>
          </div>
          <div class="bm-bento-card bm-bento-card--dark-pattern">
            <h3>No Glass Replacement Required</h3>
            <p>Blast mitigation film installs on existing glass — no removal or replacement required. The retrofit path controls cost while improving fragment retention, and most Philadelphia installations require no additional glass work.</p>
            <span class="bm-bento-card__micro-pill">Retrofit Protection</span>
          </div>
          <div class="bm-bento-card bm-bento-card--retail">
            <img src="/images/bomb-blast-film-for-philadelphia-buildings.jpg" alt="Technical badge for cost-effective blast hazard mitigation in Philadelphia buildings" title="WBDG-cited cost-effective blast mitigation film Philadelphia" loading="lazy" class="bm-bento-card__img" />
            <div class="bm-bento-card__retail-overlay"></div>
            <div class="bm-bento-card__retail-content">
              <div class="bm-bento-card__retail-badge">WBDG Cited</div>
              <h3>Cost-Effective Hazard Mitigation</h3>
              <p>Existing buildings can improve blast hazard performance without rebuilding the glazing system.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bm-masonry">
      <div class="bm-masonry__header">
        <span class="bm-masonry__eyebrow">Our Work</span>
        <h2 class="bm-masonry__title">Blast Film Installed Where Glass Risk Is Highest</h2>
        <p class="bm-masonry__sub">Commercial, civic, medical, transit, and hospitality applications across Philadelphia.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bm-masonry__grid">
        <div class="bm-masonry__item"><img src="/images/bomb-blast-window-film-installation-philadelphia.jpg" alt="Blast film installation on office tower glass in Center City Philadelphia" title="Bomb blast window film installation Philadelphia office tower" loading="lazy" /><div class="bm-masonry__caption">Blast film installation — Office Tower, Center City Philadelphia</div></div>
        <div class="bm-masonry__item"><img src="/images/blast-mitigation-film-philadelphia-government.jpg" alt="Government building blast protection in Center City Philadelphia" title="Government building blast mitigation film Philadelphia" loading="lazy" /><div class="bm-masonry__caption">Government building blast protection — Center City Philadelphia</div></div>
        <div class="bm-masonry__item"><img src="/images/bomb-blast-window-film-philadelphia-high-rise.jpg" alt="Philadelphia high-rise commercial building with bomb blast window film in Philadelphia" title="Bomb blast window film in Philadelphia high-rise building" loading="lazy" /><div class="bm-masonry__caption">Philadelphia high-rise commercial building — blast mitigation film</div></div>
        <div class="bm-masonry__item"><img src="/images/blast-mitigation-film-philadelphia-hospital.jpg" alt="Hospital building blast mitigation film in University City Philadelphia" title="Hospital blast mitigation film Philadelphia" loading="lazy" /><div class="bm-masonry__caption">Hospital building blast film — University City Philadelphia</div></div>
        <div class="bm-masonry__item"><img src="/images/bomb-blast-film-philadelphia-transit.jpg" alt="Transit hub security film protecting Philadelphia public glass" title="Transit hub bomb blast film Philadelphia" loading="lazy" /><div class="bm-masonry__caption">Transit hub security film — Philadelphia</div></div>
        <div class="bm-masonry__item"><img src="/images/blast-mitigation-window-film-philadelphia-hotel.jpg" alt="Hotel lobby blast film in Center City Philadelphia" title="Hotel lobby blast mitigation window film Philadelphia" loading="lazy" /><div class="bm-masonry__caption">Hotel lobby blast film — Center City Philadelphia</div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bm-panels">
      <div class="bm-panels__heading">
